1.1.text:
Article 3

1. The implementation of protection for women and child victims of violence aims to:
a. prevent all forms of violence;
b. protect victims of violence;
c. prosecute perpetrators of violence; and
d. maintain the integrity of harmonious and prosperous households.
2. Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. economic neglect; and
e. restriction of movement.

Article 17

1. MSS as referred to in Article 16 have performance indicators including:
a. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers within the integrated service unit;
b. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive health services by trained health personnel at community health centers and hospitals;
c. coverage of social rehabilitation services provided by trained social rehabilitation officers for women and children victims of violence within the PPT;
d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for women and children victims of violence within the integrated service unit;
e. coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level to court decisions on cases of violence against women and children;
f. coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive legal assistance;
g. coverage of repatriation services for women and children victims of violence; and
h. coverage of social reintegration services for women and children victims of violence.
2. Further provisions regarding MSS as referred to in paragraph (1) are regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 6

1. The Regional Government implements efforts to prevent the occurrence of violence against women and children, by means of:
a. collecting data and information about women and children victims of violence;
b. conducting education on anti-violence values towards women and children;
c. disseminating laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence; and
d. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence.
2. To anticipate the occurrence of violence, the Regional Government is obliged to provide and organize services for women and children victims of violence by means of:
a. establishing and facilitating the operation of integrated service institutions for victims by involving community elements;
b. encouraging public awareness of the importance of protection for women and children victims of violence;
c. conducting continuous handling up to the rehabilitation and social reintegration stage.
3. The Regional Government in implementing efforts to prevent and anticipate violence against women and children as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) must pay attention to the rights and obligations of the community, family and/or parents who are legally responsible for the victim.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The implementation of protection for women and child victims of violence is carried out based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. victim protection;
e. best interests of victims; and
f. empowerment.

Article 13

1. Forms of protection services for women and children victims of violence organized by PPT include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of the victim carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medico-legal services constitute a form of medical service for the purposes of evidence in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services constitute services provided by companions in order to recover the traumatic condition of the victim, including the provision of space and shelter for victims to protect victims from threats and intimidation;
d. legal services to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process;
e. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.
2. Further provisions regarding service mechanisms as referred to in paragraph (1) are organized according to Standard Operating Procedures regulated by Mayor Regulation.

CHAPTER V

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 9

Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to:
a. receive protection from family, police, prosecution, court, advocates, social institutions, community, Regional Government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on a protection order from the court;
b. health and psychological services according to medical needs;
c. special handling related to the confidentiality of the victim;
d. accompaniment by social workers and legal assistance at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
e. spiritual guidance services;
f. the right to information on case developments;
g. the right to shelter services;
h. the right to restitution;
i. the right to psychosocial services/recovery; and
j. the right to social reintegration.

REGIONAL REGULATION OF MADIUN CITY

NUMBER 9 OF 2015

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF MADIUN,

Considering:

a. that forms of violence against women and children constitute a form of violation of human rights based on Pancasila and the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
b. that acts of violence against women and children will have prolonged physical, psychological, sexual, social and economic impacts, therefore it is necessary to provide a sense of security and protection;
c. that at present there is no Regional Regulation that guarantees protection for women and children victims of violence in Madiun City;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 16 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Large City Regions Within the Provinces of East Java, Central Java, West Java and the Special Region of Yogyakarta (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950 Number 45);
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);
5. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
6. Government Regulation Number 49 of 1982 concerning Amendment to the Territorial Boundaries of the Second Level Municipality Region of Madiun (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1982 Number 76, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3244);
7. Presidential Regulation Number 18 of 2014 concerning Protection and Empowerment of Women and Children in Social Conflict;
8. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
9. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Handling Child Victims of Violence;
10. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence;
11. Regional Regulation of East Java Province Number 16 of 2012 concerning Implementation of Protection for Women and Children Victims of Violence (Regional Gazette of East Java Province of 2012 Number 3 Series D);
12. Regional Regulation of Madiun City Number 02 of 2009 concerning Civil Servant Investigators within the Madiun City Government (Regional Gazette of Madiun City of 2009 Number 1/E);

With Joint Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF MADIUN CITY

and

MAYOR OF MADIUN

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE.

CHAPTER XII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 26

1. In the implementation of protection for women and child victims of violence, the community may:
a. form family partners at the Kelurahan level by the community;
b. conduct socialization of the rights of women and children independently;
c. provide first aid to victims; and
d. report to the competent authority if violence against women and children occurs in their environment.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:
a. individuals;
b. social community institutions;
c. non-governmental organizations;
d. educational institutions;
e. religious institutions;
f. private sector; and/or
g. mass media.

